<chapter xml:id="ch-reasoning-types" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Reasoning Types</title>
  <intro>
    <p>
      Understanding these reasoning patterns allows us to diagnose our own cognitive processes when building Eigenotes.
      We employ deductive reasoning to derive consequences from axioms, abductive reasoning to generate hypotheses from anomalous data, and spatial reasoning to visualize geometric structures.
    </p>
  </intro>

  <table xml:id="tab-reasoning-types">
    <title>Classification of Reasoning Types</title>
    <idx>deductive reasoning</idx>
    <idx>inductive reasoning</idx>
    <idx>temporal reasoning</idx>
    <idx>spatial reasoning</idx>
    <idx>causal reasoning</idx>
    <idx>comparative analysis</idx>
    <idx>abstract reasoning</idx>
    <idx>pattern recognition</idx>
    <idx>statistical reasoning</idx>
    <idx>abductive reasoning</idx>
    <idx>hypothetical reasoning</idx>

    <tabular bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col width="30%"/>
      <col width="40%"/>
      <col width="30%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Reasoning Type</cell>
        <cell>Definition</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Deductive reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Drawing specific conclusions from general laws or principles.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>Given this mechanisms, what must follow?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Inductive reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Generalizing from patterns or experimental observations.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>Given this data, what can we infer about X?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Temporal reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Predicting events or states based on order in time.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>If A happens before B, and B affects C, what's the downstream effect?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Spatial reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Understanding structures, orientation, or symmetry.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>What is the normal force on an object sliding down a ramp inclined at an angle X?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Causal reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Identifying cause-and-effect relationships.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>What arrangement for three electrons causes zero electric field at the center-of-mass location?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Comparative analysis</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Judging between alternatives or evaluating differences across conditions.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>What is the percent difference in the ground-state energy of an atom trapped in a harmonic potential as opposed to a quartic potential?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Abstract reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Working with non-concrete or theoretical ideas.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>What's the entropy change in a hypothetical closed system with constraint X?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Pattern recognition</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Spotting and interpreting regularities in data or sequences.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>The amounts of a radioactive isotope at times <m>t_1</m>, <m>t_2</m>, and <m>t_3</m> are <m>N_1</m>, <m>N_2</m>, and <m>N_3</m>. What is the half-life of the isotope?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Statistical reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Using data, probabilities, and distributions to reach conclusions.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>Determine the uncertainty in the extracted value of X given the set of experimental data.</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Abductive reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Inferring the most likely explanation from incomplete evidence
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>As the temperature of a material changes from <m>T_1</m> to <m>T_2</m>, the spatial correlation length increases exponentially. What is likely true about the location of the material in phase-space (it is near a critical point/phase transition)?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <term>Hypothetical Reasoning</term>
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Predicting outcomes under hypothetical or counterfactual scenarios.
          </p>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            <q>What would be the fractional change in the Earth's gravitational field if the gravitational constant was doubled in magnitude?</q>
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>


    </tabular>
  </table>


</chapter>